const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"LR_E2A_Q001","exercise":"exercise_2a","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":false,"question_text":"Eight boys A, B, C, D, E, F, G and H sit around a circular table, not necessarily in the same order. B and D sit neither adjacent to C nor opposite to C. A sits in between E and D, and F sits in between B and H. Which of the following is definitely true?","options":{"A":"H sits in between C and E.","B":"B sits in between A and G.","C":"C sits opposite G.","D":"None of these"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"None of these","solution_text":"It is given that A sits in between E and D and F sits in between B and H. It is also given that B and D sit neither adjacent to C nor opposite to C. [Diagram shows three possible arrangements being tested.] Hence, none of the choices is definitely true.","final_arrangement":"G A B C E F D H (circular order)","entity_count":8,"variables":1,"constraints":4,"case_splits":3,"resolution_type":"multiple","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["case_elimination","trial_and_error_verification"],"question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"set_id":"LR_E2A_S2_4","exercise":"exercise_2a","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Eight people A, B, C, D, E, F, G, and H are sitting around a rectangular table not necessarily in the same order. One person sits along the shorter side and three persons sit along the longer side. A and G are sitting diagonally opposite to each other. D and E are sitting opposite to each other. A is sitting to the immediate left of F, who is sitting at one of the shorter sides of the table.","setup_solution":"It is given that eight people – A, B, C, D, E, F, G and H are sitting around a rectangular table. One sits along the shorter side and three sit along the longer side. A and G are sitting diagonally opposite each other and A is sitting to the immediate left of F. F is sitting along the shorter side. D and E are sitting opposite each other.","entity_count":8,"variables":2,"constraints":4,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"multiple","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["multi_constraint_balancing","case_elimination"],"type_specific":{"table_shape":"rectangular","note":"Non-standard table geometry: shorter side seats 1 person, longer sides seat 3 each. Diagonal-opposite and side-opposite relationships are distinct, adding a spatial-reasoning layer beyond standard circular adjacency/opposite logic."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E2A_Q002","question_text":"If C is sitting to the immediate right of H, then who is sitting opposite to F?","options":{"A":"B","B":"C","C":"H","D":"D"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"H","solution_text":"If C is sitting to the immediate right of H: H sits opposite to F.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q003","question_text":"If C is sitting opposite to A, then who among the following must be sitting on the same side as C?","options":{"A":"G and E","B":"G and D","C":"G","D":"H or B"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"G","solution_text":"If C is sitting opposite A, then the arrangement is as follows: So, G sits on the same side as C.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q004","question_text":"If H is not sitting at one of the shorter sides, then how many arrangements are possible?","options":{"A":"24","B":"8","C":"4","D":"16"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"8","solution_text":"If H is not sitting at one of the shorter sides, then the arrangement is as follows: H is seated in two ways. B and C are seated in two ways. D and E are seated in two ways. Total ways = 2 × 2 × 2 = 8","question_operation":"count_arrangements","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E2A_S5_7","exercise":"exercise_2a","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Sameer, Sameep, Sandeep, Sangeet, Sanskar and Saarang are sitting around a hexagonal table in a library studying a book each. The cover of the book in their hands is of different colours from among – Red, Green, Blue, Orange, White and Yellow – not necessarily in that order. Further, it is known that Sameer sits opposite to Sameep, who sits to the immediate left of the person holding the Blue cover book, who sits two places away from the person holding the White cover book. Sangeet sits two places away from the person holding the Yellow cover book and sits opposite to the person holding the white cover book. Sandeep and Saarang sit next to each other.","setup_solution":"Based on Sameer opposite Sameep, Sameep immediate left of Blue, Blue two places from White, we get arrangements where the White-holder may be at seat 5 or seat 1 relative to Sameer/Sameep. From the condition that Sangeet is opposite White, Sameer cannot have the White book (since he is opposite Sandeep's seat area, not Sangeet's), so the White book holder must be immediate left of Sameep, i.e. at seat 5. Then Sangeet is opposite White and to the left of Sameer, at seat 2. Since Sandeep and Saarang are adjacent, they occupy seats 5 and 6 in any order. The only remaining seat for Sanskar is seat 3. Final arrangement (seats 1-6 clockwise): Sameer(1), Sangeet(2)-Green, Sanskar(3)-Blue, Sameep(4), Sandeep/Saarang(5)-White, Saarang/Sandeep(6).","entity_count":6,"variables":2,"constraints":5,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"multiple","table_geometry":{"shape":"hexagonal","seats":6},"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["multi_constraint_balancing","case_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E2A_Q005","question_text":"What must be the colour of the book with Sanskar?","options":{"A":"Yellow","B":"White","C":"Blue","D":"Red"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Blue","solution_text":"Hence, Sanskar holds the Blue cover book in his hand.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q006","question_text":"If the person holding the Orange cover book sits opposite to the person holding the Red cover Book, then which statement among the following cannot be true?","options":{"A":"Sanskar does not hold the White cover book.","B":"Sangeet is not holding a Green cover book.","C":"Saarang holds the Yellow cover book.","D":"Sandeep holds the Yellow cover book."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Sangeet is not holding a Green cover book.","solution_text":"In the above figure, if the person sitting at seat no. 4 (i.e., Sameep) holds Yellow cover book, then the Red and Orange coloured books cannot be opposite to each other. Hence, the person sitting at seat no.6 (i.e., either Saarang or Sandeep) must have Yellow cover book and Sameer and Sameep will have Red cover and Orange cover book in any order. Then, we get the following arrangement. Hence, Sangeet must have Green book. Choice (B) is the only one that is definitely false.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q007","question_text":"If Sandeep sits opposite the person holding the Green cover book then who is sitting opposite the person holding the Red cover book?","options":{"A":"Sandeep","B":"Sameer","C":"Sanskar","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Cannot be determined","solution_text":"Sandeep is sitting opposite the person holding the Green cover book which implies that Sandeep is sitting opposite Sangeet. Thus, either Sameer or Sandeep holds the Red cover book. Hence, it cannot be determined.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E2A_S8_10","exercise":"exercise_2a","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Eight persons – Arun, Pankaj, Rohan, Veda, Suman, Shanu, Dimple and Pinky are sitting around a circular table for a group discussion. Suman is not sitting opposite Pinky and Shanu is sitting three places away to the right of Pankaj. Dimple is sitting in between Pankaj and Suman. Rohan is sitting adjacent to Pankaj who is sitting opposite Arun.","setup_solution":"It is given that Rohan is sitting adjacent to Pankaj who is sitting opposite Arun. From the above arrangements and the remaining information, we get the following two arrangements: Case (i): Arun, Rohan, Pankaj, Dimple, Suman, Shanu, Pinky, Veda (going around). Case (ii): Arun, Rohan, Pankaj, Dimple, Suman, Shanu, Veda, Pinky (going around).","entity_count":8,"variables":1,"constraints":4,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"multiple","table_geometry":{"shape":"circular","seats":8},"information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["linear_sequencing","case_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E2A_Q008","question_text":"Who is sitting opposite Dimple?","options":{"A":"Piniky","B":"Shanu","C":"Rohan","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Cannot be determined","solution_text":"Either Pinky or Shanu sits opposite Dimple.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q009","question_text":"Who is sitting opposite Veda?","options":{"A":"Suman","B":"Pinky","C":"Shanu","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Suman","solution_text":"Suman is sitting opposite Veda.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q010","question_text":"If Rohan is sitting to the left of Veda, then who is sitting opposite Shanu?","options":{"A":"Rohan","B":"Dimple","C":"Suman","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Dimple","solution_text":"In case (i) Dimple is sitting opposite Shanu.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E2A_S11_13","exercise":"exercise_2a","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Each of the six persons – John, Ted, Humpty, Dumpty, Jack and Jill is from one different country among India, Japan, China, Australia, America and England and are sitting around a circular table, may not be in the same order. John, who is from China is sitting adjacent to an American, who is not Humpty. Ted is not an Indian, and the Chinese is not sitting adjacent to the Indian. The person from England is sitting one place away to the left of the Australian. Humpty is sitting opposite to the Indian, who is adjacent to the Japanese. The Australian and Dumpty are sitting opposite each other. Jack is not from India and Ted is not from Japan and both are not adjacent to each other.","setup_solution":"From the given information John is from China and is adjacent to American who is not Humpty. But Humpty is opposite the Indian who is not adjacent to Chinese but adjacent to Japanese, thus two arrangements are possible (Case i and Case ii). The English man is left to Australian; in case (i) Humpty can be from England, in case (ii) Humpty can be from England or Australia. As Australian is opposite Dumpty: in Case (i), Dumpty is the American; in Case (ii), Dumpty is the Indian. In Case (i), among the remaining people (Ted, Jill, Jack), Ted and Jack are not from India so Jill is the Indian; Ted and Jack would be Australian and Japanese and sitting adjacent to each other, which is not possible — so Case (i) is eliminated. In Case (ii): either Jack or Jill is from Japan. If Jack is from Japan, Ted is the American and Jill is the English. If Jill is the Japanese, then Ted is either England or American and Jack is either England or America. Final: John-China, Humpty-Australia, Dumpty-India, and among Ted/Jack/Jill: Japan/America/England as derived per sub-case.","entity_count":6,"variables":2,"constraints":7,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"multiple","table_geometry":{"shape":"circular","seats":6},"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["case_elimination","multi_constraint_balancing"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E2A_Q011","question_text":"Who among them is from India?","options":{"A":"Jill","B":"Dumpty","C":"Humpty","D":"None of these"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Dumpty","solution_text":"Dumpty is from India.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q012","question_text":"If Jack is the Japanese, then who is sitting opposite the American?","options":{"A":"Jill","B":"Ted","C":"Jack","D":"Dumpty"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Jill","solution_text":"Jill is sitting opposite the American.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q013","question_text":"Which country does Humpty belong to?","options":{"A":"Japan","B":"Australia","C":"America","D":"England"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Australia","solution_text":"Humpty belongs to Australia.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E2A_S14_16","exercise":"exercise_2a","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Eight friends, A through H, are sitting around a circular table, playing a game of cards. They belong to two different teams X and Y. No two persons of the same team sit in adjacent seats. (i) A sits neither opposite to D nor to H but is sitting in between C and G. (ii) B sits neither opposite to A nor to G but is sitting in between F and D. (iii) B and H belong to team X and D sits opposite to E. (iv) A scored two points more than D, who scored three points more than F, who scored four points more than E. B scored twice as that of G, who scored twice that of C, who scored twice that of H.","setup_solution":"From (1), A sits between C and G but not opposite D or H. From (1), (2) and (3) we get the circular arrangement: going around – C, A, G, F, D, B, E, H (or its mirror E, B, D, F, G, A, C, H), with D opposite E. Let the points scored by E = x, F = x + 4, D = x + 7 and A = x + 9. Similarly, let the points scored by H = y, C = 2y, G = 4y and B = 8y.","entity_count":8,"variables":2,"constraints":4,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","table_geometry":{"shape":"circular","seats":8},"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["multi_constraint_balancing","linear_sequencing"],"type_specific":{"note":"Combines seating logic (constraint i-iii) with an independent algebraic scoring system (constraint iv) expressed as ratios/differences rather than direct values. Two distinct sub-skills: positional deduction and algebraic relationship solving, evaluated somewhat independently per question."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E2A_Q014","question_text":"Who are the members of team X?","options":{"A":"A, D, F and E","B":"B, H, C and E","C":"B, D, H and G","D":"B, H, C and G"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"B, H, C and G","solution_text":"B, H, C and G are the members of team X.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q015","question_text":"If E and H scored one point each, then which team wins the game, given that the team with the minimum points wins the game?","options":{"A":"Team X","B":"Team Y","C":"Both teams scored the same points","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Team X","solution_text":"If E scores 1 point, then F scores 5 points, D scores 8 points and A scores 10 points. A, D, E and F belong to team Y and their total points is 1 + 5 + 8 + 10 = 24 points. Similarly H scores 1, C scores 2, G scores 4 and B scores 8. B, C, G and H belong to team X and the total sum of their points is 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 15 points. Hence, team X wins the game.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q016","question_text":"If team Y scores 92 points and team X scores 90 points, then which among the following is definitely true?","options":{"A":"H and D scores equal points.","B":"B scores twice as many points as F.","C":"A scores three points less that G.","D":"D scores one point more than G."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"D scores one point more than G.","solution_text":"If team Y scores 92 points means E + F + D + A = 92. x + (x + 4) + (x + 7) + (x + 9) = 92. 4x + 20 = 92, x = 18. E scores 18, F scores 22, D scores 25 and A scores 27. If team X scores 90 points, then y + 2y + 4y + 8y = 90, 15y = 90, y = 6. H scores 6, C scores 12, G scores 24 and B scores 48. D scores one point more than G is definitely true.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150}]},{"set_id":"LR_E2A_S17_19","exercise":"exercise_2a","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Six boys – Prasad, Prakash, Prashant, Pranay, Praveen and Prabhat − each wearing a T-shirt of a different colour among Indigo, Green, Blue, Orange, Violet and Yellow, are sitting around a table in six equi-spaced chairs. Prakash is opposite to the boy wearing the Orange T-Shirt. Prabhat is opposite to the boy wearing the Green T-Shirt. Prashant is to the right of the boy wearing the Indigo T-Shirt and opposite the boy wearing the Violet T-Shirt. Praveen is between the boys wearing Orange and Yellow T-Shirts and is not wearing the Violet T-Shirt. Pranay is opposite the boy who is wearing the Yellow T-Shirt.","setup_solution":"By taking the data given in the problem, we get four different arrangements (the first letters of the colour names are used to denote the colours). Case 1: Prabhat(B), Prasad(O), Praveen(G), Prashant(Y), Prakash(I), Pranay(V) around the table. Case 2: Prabhat(B), Prasad(Y), Praveen(G), Prashant(O), Prakash(V), Pranay(I) around the table. Case 3: Prabhat(O), Prasad(B), Praveen(I), Prashant(Y), Prakash(G), Pranay(V) around the table. Case 4: Prabhat(Y), Prasad(B), Praveen(I), Prashant(O), Prakash(G), Pranay(V) around the table.","entity_count":6,"variables":1,"constraints":5,"case_splits":4,"resolution_type":"multiple","table_geometry":{"shape":"circular","seats":6},"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["case_elimination","multi_constraint_balancing"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E2A_Q017","question_text":"Which of the following statements is true? I. The boys wearing Green and Orange T-Shirts are either next to each other or opposite to each other. II. The boys wearing Indigo and Orange T-Shirts are either next to each other or opposite to each other. III. The boys wearing Blue and Violet T-Shirts are always next to each other. IV. The boys wearing Blue and Indigo T-Shirts are either opposite to each other or are next to each other.","options":{"A":"Only I and II","B":"Only II","C":"Only II and IV","D":"I, II, III and IV"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"I, II, III and IV","solution_text":"All the statements are true.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q018","question_text":"If Prabhat is wearing the Orange T-Shirt, then who is wearing the Green T-Shirt?","options":{"A":"Prasad","B":"Prakash","C":"Praveen","D":"Pranay"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Prakash","solution_text":"This question is referring to case-3. In this case, it is Prakash who is wearing the Green T-shirt.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q019","question_text":"If Prashant is wearing the Orange T-Shirt, then who is between Praveen and Pranay?","options":{"A":"Prashant","B":"Prasad","C":"Prakash","D":"Prabhat"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Prashant","solution_text":"This question is referring to cases-2 and 4. In both the cases it is Prashant who is between Praveen and Pranay.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E2A_S20_22","exercise":"exercise_2a","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Eight persons − Ram, Ramesh, Mohan, Sohan, Seema, Saroj, Sakshi and Saloni are sitting around a circular table. Each of them is one among Doctor, Engineer, Dancer, Singer, Teacher, Lawyer, Accountant and Pilot, not necessarily in the given order. Further it is known that I. Pilot is sitting opposite Ramesh, who is adjacent to the Accountant. II. Dancer is sitting opposite the Lawyer and is not adjacent to Sakshi who is not sitting adjacent to the Lawyer. III. Saloni is sitting opposite the Engineer, Ramesh is not a Lawyer or Doctor or Engineer. IV. Sakshi, the Singer, is sitting one place away to the right of Saroj. V. Seema is sitting opposite the Lawyer and Ram is sitting opposite the Dancer. VI. Ramesh is sitting three places to the right of Singer. Mohan is neither the Accountant nor adjacent to the Dancer.","setup_solution":"From II and V, Seema, the Dancer is opposite Ram, who is the Lawyer, and Sakshi is not adjacent to anyone of these two. From IV, Sakshi, who is the Singer, is one place to the right of Saroj. From VI, Ramesh is sitting three places to the right of the Singer. From I, Pilot is sitting opposite Ramesh, thus Saroj is the Pilot, and from III, Saloni is opposite the Engineer. From I and the above arrangement, Ramesh cannot be anyone except Teacher. Saloni is the Doctor, Mohan is the Engineer and Sohan is the Accountant.","entity_count":8,"variables":2,"constraints":6,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","table_geometry":{"shape":"circular","seats":8},"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["multi_constraint_balancing","linear_sequencing"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E2A_Q020","question_text":"Who among the following is the Doctor?","options":{"A":"Ramesh","B":"Saloni","C":"Saroj","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Saloni","solution_text":"Saloni is the Doctor.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q021","question_text":"What is the profession of Mohan?","options":{"A":"Accountant","B":"Pilot","C":"Engineer","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Engineer","solution_text":"Mohan is the Engineer.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q022","question_text":"Who is sitting opposite Ramesh?","options":{"A":"Seema","B":"Sakshi","C":"Saroj","D":"None of these"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Saroj","solution_text":"Saroj is sitting opposite Ramesh.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E2A_S23_26","exercise":"exercise_2a","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Eight persons – K, L, M, N, O, P, Q, and R – sit around a circular table not necessarily in the same order. Some of them are facing the centre and the remaining are facing away from the centre. (i) P sits to the immediate left of K. (ii) M and R are sitting in the opposite places. (iii) Either 'Q' or 'O' but not both sits next to L. (iv) No two persons sitting next to each other face the same direction. (v) R is the neighbour of both N and Q. (vi) L sits opposite K and faces away from the centre.","setup_solution":"From (vi), L sits opposite K and faces away from the centre. From (iv), no two persons sitting next to each other face the same direction, and from (i), P sits to the immediate left of K. From these points we get an initial arrangement of L, K, P with directions alternating. From (ii), M and R are sitting opposite each other. From (v), R is the neighbour of both N and Q. From (iii), either Q or O sits next to L. Final arrangement (clockwise): L, K, P, M, Q, N, R, O — with facing directions alternating around the table per condition (iv), starting from L facing away from centre.","entity_count":8,"variables":2,"constraints":6,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","table_geometry":{"shape":"circular","seats":8},"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["linear_sequencing","multi_constraint_balancing"],"type_specific":{"note":"Facing direction (centre vs away) tracked as a second variable alongside position. Alternating-direction constraint (iv) functions similarly to an adjacency-pattern rule and must be checked at every placement step."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E2A_Q023","question_text":"Who is sitting opposite N?","options":{"A":"M","B":"O","C":"Q","D":"P"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"P","solution_text":"P sits opposite N.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q024","question_text":"Three of the following four are alike in a certain way and so form a group. Which is the one that does not belong to that group?","options":{"A":"KM","B":"ML","C":"NO","D":"PO"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"PO","solution_text":"Among KM, ML and NO, the second person sits second to the left of the first person. In PO, the second person is second to the right of the first person.","question_operation":"odd_one_out","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q025","question_text":"Which among the following is 'definitely true'?","options":{"A":"P is facing away from the centre.","B":"M and N are opposite each other.","C":"K sits to the immediate right of P.","D":"L sits to the immediate right of O."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"L sits to the immediate right of O.","solution_text":"L sits to the immediate right of 'O' is definitely true.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q026","question_text":"If M and N interchange their places, then who among the following sits to the immediate left of M?","options":{"A":"O","B":"P","C":"L","D":"R"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"L","solution_text":"If M and N interchange their places, L sits to the immediate left of M.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E2A_S27_30","exercise":"exercise_2a","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Among seven persons – Tanuja, Divya and Vasudha are females and Srikanth, Ganesh, Sateesh and Appu are males and they are sitting around a circular table, but not necessarily in the same order. The following information is known about their seating. No two females are adjacent to each other. Ganesh and Appu are not adjacent to each other. Srikanth is sitting to the immediate left of Vasudha, who is third to the left of Appu. Tanuja is not adjacent to Sateesh. Appu and Sateesh are adjacent to each other. All are facing the center.","setup_solution":"It is given that no two females are adjacent to each other. Vasudha (female) is third to the left of Appu (male) and Srikanth is to the immediate left of Vasudha. Ganesh and Appu are not adjacent to each other. Hence, the arrangement will be: Srikanth, Vasudha, Ganesh ... Appu (with gaps to fill). Appu and Sateesh are adjacent to each other, but Tanuja is not adjacent to Sateesh. Hence, the final arrangement (clockwise): Srikanth, Vasudha, Divya, Sateesh, Appu, Tanuja, Ganesh (going around the circle back to Srikanth).","entity_count":7,"variables":2,"constraints":5,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","table_geometry":{"shape":"circular","seats":7},"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["multi_constraint_balancing","linear_sequencing"],"type_specific":{"note":"Odd entity count (7) with a gender-alternation-style constraint (no two females adjacent) layered on top of direct positional clues. Gender tracked as second variable alongside position."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E2A_Q027","question_text":"Who is sitting second to the right of Appu?","options":{"A":"Divya","B":"Tanuja","C":"Srikanth","D":"Ganesh"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Divya","solution_text":"Divya is second to the right of Appu.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q028","question_text":"Who among the following is sitting adjacent to Vasudha?","options":{"A":"Sateesh","B":"Appu","C":"Divya","D":"Ganesh"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Ganesh","solution_text":"Ganesh is sitting adjacent to Vasudha.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q029","question_text":"In a certain way, Vasudha is related to Divya. Sateesh is related to whom in the same way?","options":{"A":"Srikanth","B":"Ganesh","C":"Tanuja","D":"Appu"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Tanuja","solution_text":"Vasudha is sitting second to the right of Divya. Similarly Sateesh is sitting second to the right of Tanuja.","question_operation":"odd_one_out","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E2A_Q030","question_text":"Three out of the following four follow a particular pattern and so form a group. Find the one which does not belong to the group.","options":{"A":"Appu, Divya","B":"Vasudha, Ganesh","C":"Divya, Vasudha","D":"Vasudha, Tanuja"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Vasudha, Ganesh","solution_text":"Except Vasudha and Ganesh, in all other options, the second person is sitting second to the right of first person.","question_operation":"odd_one_out","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E2B_S1_3","exercise":"exercise_2b","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":true,"setup_text":"There are six managers in a company. Akhil, Balwant, Chetan, Dhawan, Eshwar and Feroz. One morning, they sat around a circular table for an important discussion. They met one another again in the evening and sat at the same table. The following is known about their seating positions: (i) In the evening, each person changed his place and no one sat at the place adjacent to the one at which he sat in the morning. (ii) No one sat opposite the same person in the evening as in the morning. (iii) Akhil was opposite to Chetan in the morning. (iv) Eshwar never sat adjacent to Chetan. (v) Dhawan sat in the opposite place in the evening. Not all sat in the opposite places in the evening. (vi) Akhil sat second to the left of Dhawan in the evening.","setup_solution":"Let us number the places from 1 to 6 for the morning, and let Dhawan be seated at 1. Also let us indicate the places by p, q, r, s, t and u in the evening. From (vi) we get the position of Akhil in the evening. Now, the person sitting at 4 can sit at p or q, from (i). From (ii) person sitting at 4 cannot be sitting at p. From (iii) Akhil is not sitting at 4. Thus, we can say that the person sitting at 4 is sitting at 'q'. Thus, the only place where the person sitting at 6 can sit is 'r'. Similarly, the person sitting at 5 can sit at 'p'. From above and (ii), we can say that the person sitting at 3 can sit at 't' and the person sitting at 2 can sit at 'u'. Thus, A is at 2. Now from (iii), Chetan is the 5th person. From (iv), Eshwar is the person sitting at 3. Thus, the persons 4 and 6 are either Balwant or Feroz. Morning order (1-6): Dhawan, Akhil, Eshwar, Balwant/Feroz, Chetan, Feroz/Balwant. Evening order (p-u, matching morning positions 1→6 to evening p,u,t,q,r,p mapping as derived): Dhawan, Chetan, Eshwar, Balwant/Feroz, Akhil, Feroz/Balwant.","entity_count":6,"variables":2,"constraints":6,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"multiple","table_geometry":{"shape":"circular","seats":6},"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["multi_constraint_balancing","linear_sequencing"],"type_specific":{"dual_state":true,"note":"Two parallel seating states (morning and evening) for the same six people at the same table, linked by transformation constraints (no one adjacent to or opposite their own prior position). Complexity driver is tracking the mapping between two arrangements simultaneously, not just resolving one arrangement."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E2B_Q001","question_text":"Who was sitting to the right of A in the evening?","options":{"A":"E","B":"C","C":"D","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"E","solution_text":"E is sitting to the right of A in the evening.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q002","question_text":"If B was sitting adjacent to E in the morning then who was sitting to the left of him in the evening?","options":{"A":"D","B":"E","C":"C","D":"F"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"F","solution_text":"If B is seated adjacent to E in the morning then B is the person at 4. Hence, F will be sitting to his left in the evening.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q003","question_text":"If F's position in the evening was not opposite to his position in the morning then which of the following is correct?","options":{"A":"F was sitting opposite to E in the morning.","B":"B was sitting between C and F in the evening.","C":"B was adjacent to D in the evening.","D":"More than one of the above"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"B was adjacent to D in the evening.","solution_text":"F's position in the evening is not opposite to his position in the morning i.e. F is in the 4th position. Thus, B is in the position 6. Hence only choice (C) is true.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150}]},{"set_id":"LR_E2B_S4_6","exercise":"exercise_2b","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Eight students A, B, C, D, E, F, G and H sit around a circular table in their college canteen. They are from different branches of study among CSE, ECE and IT. Each of them drinks a different cold drink among Cola, Dew, Limca, Mazza, Pepsi, Slice, Sprite and Thumbs Up, though not necessarily in the same order. Further it is known that, (i) Among the students, three are from CSE, two are from IT and the rest are from ECE. (ii) No two students from the same branch sit adjacent to each other. (iii) The students who drink Pepsi, Cola, and Sprite sit in adjacent positions in some order. (iv) H is from IT and sits opposite to the person who drink Slice. (v) The student who drink Thumbs Up does not sit adjacent to a student from ECE. (vi) A is from CSE and G drink Slice. (vii) D and the person who sits opposite him are from the same branch. (viii) F is from ECE and sits to the left of E who is adjacent to A. (ix) C drinks Mazza, and sits opposite A. (x) A drinks Thumbs Up. E does not drink Limca.","setup_solution":"From (i) we have, CSE – 3, ECE – 3, and IT – 2. From (vi) and (x) A drinks Thumbs up and he is from CSE. From (v), A is not adjacent to a student from ECE. Thus, the persons adjacent to A are from IT. From the above and clue (ii) we get the arrangement. From (vii), D is from ECE. From (ix), C is opposite to A, he drinks Mazza and he is from ECE. From (viii), as F is from ECE, he must be opposite to D, and F must be second to the left of A. From (iv), H is to the immediate right of A and he is opposite to the person who drinks Slice, who is G (from vi). Thus, B is to the immediate left of C. From (iii), B, D and H drink Pepsi, Cola and Sprite (not necessarily in that order). From (x), F had Limca and E had Dew. Final arrangement (clockwise around table): A(CSE,Thumbs Up), H(IT), G(Slice), C(ECE,Mazza), D(ECE), F(ECE,Limca), E(Dew), B — with B,D,H holding Pepsi/Cola/Sprite in some order.","entity_count":8,"variables":3,"constraints":10,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","table_geometry":{"shape":"circular","seats":8},"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["multi_constraint_balancing","linear_sequencing"],"type_specific":{"attribute_layers":2,"note":"Three independent attributes per person: position, branch (CSE/ECE/IT), and drink. Branch follows a non-adjacency rule similar to a gender-alternation constraint. High constraint count (10) relative to entity count makes this a dense, low case-split but high information-load set."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E2B_Q004","question_text":"The students who sit adjacent to D are","options":{"A":"B and H","B":"E and G","C":"H and G","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"B and H","solution_text":"B and H sit adjacent to D.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q005","question_text":"Which of the following is the correct combination of the students, his branch of study and his drink?","options":{"A":"E – ECE – Dew","B":"H – IT – Pepsi","C":"F – ECE – Limca","D":"More than one of the above"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"F – ECE – Limca","solution_text":"F – ECE – Limca is the correct combination.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q006","question_text":"If the student from ECE drinks Pepsi and the student adjacent to the person from CSE drinks Cola then the student sitting opposite the one drinking Sprite has which drink?","options":{"A":"Dew","B":"Limca","C":"Slice","D":"Data inconsistent"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Dew","solution_text":"D is from ECE thus he drinks Pepsi. H is adjacent to CSE thus he drinks Cola. Hence B had Sprite. The person opposite to B is E who drinks Dew.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150}]},{"set_id":"LR_E2B_S7_11","exercise":"exercise_2b","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Eight persons – L, M, N, O, P, Q, R and S – are sitting around a circular table facing the centre, but not necessarily in the same order. No two persons whose names starts with successive letters of the English alphabet sit adjacent to each other. For example: L does not sit adjacent to M, M sits adjacent to neither L nor N and so on. Each person has a different CIBIL score which is a multiple of 50. CIBIL score is divided into four categories among – Poor – The one who has CIBIL score between 300 to 550 (both values included), Average – The one who has CIBIL score between 551 to 650 (both values included), Good – The one who has CIBIL score between 651 to 750 (both values included) and Excellent – The one who has CIBIL score between 751 to 850 (both values included). Each person CIBIL score lies between 300 to 850. The following information is known about them. Only one person sits between M and S and the average score of M and S is 325. P sits to the immediate right of S. The sum of the score of P and S is 1000. P's score is not good. The number of people who sit between M and P when counted to the left of P is the same as the number of people who sit between S and L when counted to the left of L. O's score is good and sits opposite the person whose score is half the score of O's. Neither R nor Q sits to the immediate left of N. L's score is average. Q's and N's scores are excellent and N's score is more than the score of Q's.","setup_solution":"Only one person sits between M and S and the average of M and S score is 325, so S sits either second to the left or second to the right of M. The only possibility of S's and M's scores are 300 and 350 in any order. P sits either to the immediate left or third to the right of M. Sum of P and S is 1000: if S=300 then P=700; if S=350 then P=650. Since P's score is not good, S=350 and P=650, so M=300. The number of people between M and P (left of P) equals number between S and L (left of L), so L sits either second to the left or second to the right of P. O's score is good and sits opposite the person whose score is half of O's: O=700, opposite person=350=S. So O sits opposite S. N sits to the immediate left of L (non-adjacency rule). Both Q and R sit adjacent to O and M in some order; since neither R nor Q sits immediate left of N, and L's score is average (600), case with R/Q to immediate left of N is eliminated, leaving Q and R to the immediate left/right of M. Q and N's scores are excellent (800, 850) with N > Q, so N=850, Q=800. Q sits to the immediate right of M; R sits to the immediate right of O. Final arrangement (clockwise): L(600), N(850), O(700), P(650), S(350), M(300), R, Q(800).","entity_count":8,"variables":2,"constraints":9,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"single","table_geometry":{"shape":"circular","seats":8},"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["multi_constraint_balancing","case_elimination"],"type_specific":{"attribute_layers":1,"note":"Position plus a numeric score attribute with derived categorical bucketing (Poor/Average/Good/Excellent). Adjacency rule based on alphabetical succession of names rather than a given/named pair, requiring the rule to be checked generically against every placement."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E2B_Q007","question_text":"If R's score is more than P's score, then how many people have their score in the poor category?","options":{"A":"Two","B":"Four","C":"Six","D":"Five"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Two","solution_text":"If R's score is more than P's score, then only two persons have their score in the poor category.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q008","question_text":"Who sits second to the right of Q?","options":{"A":"P","B":"S","C":"L","D":"R"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"P","solution_text":"P sits second to the right of Q.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q009","question_text":"How many people's scores are more than O's?","options":{"A":"Three","B":"Two","C":"Five","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Cannot be determined","solution_text":"Since we do not have any information about R's score, hence cannot be determined.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q010","question_text":"What is P's score categorised as?","options":{"A":"Good","B":"Average","C":"Poor","D":"Excellent"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Average","solution_text":"P's score is categorised as average.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q011","question_text":"Who sits opposite M?","options":{"A":"P","B":"R","C":"N","D":"S"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"N","solution_text":"N sits opposite M.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75}]},{"set_id":"LR_E2B_S12_14","exercise":"exercise_2b","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Twelve persons – J, K, L, M, N, O, P, Q, R, S, T and U are sitting around a circle in 12 different positions as the hour divisions in a clock. The following information is known about their seating. 1. P is opposite U who is not at 60° from T. 2. N is at 90° from S in anticlockwise direction. 3. U is to the left of L, who is at 180° from R. 4. R is at 90° from T and is at 30° from Q. 5. K is at 60° from O. 6. J is not at 90° from R. 7. S is at the 4th hour division of the clock. 8. P is at 30° from N.","setup_solution":"From (1), (2), (7) and (8), we get two possible arrangements (Case i and Case ii). Condition (3) cannot be satisfied in case (i). Hence, only case (ii) prevails. From (4), T is at the 2nd hour mark and Q is at the 10th hour mark. From (5), K and O are at the 7th and the 9th hour marks in any order. From (6), J is at the 3rd hour mark. Hence, M is at the 8th hour mark. Final arrangement by hour position (1-12 clockwise): position assignments derived as S=4, T=2, Q=10, J=3, M=8, with K/O at 7/9 in either order, and remaining positions (1,5,6,11,12) for P, U, N, L, R per conditions 1, 2, 3, 8.","entity_count":12,"variables":1,"constraints":8,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"single","table_geometry":{"shape":"circular_clock","seats":12},"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["case_elimination","linear_sequencing"],"type_specific":{"angular_reasoning":true,"note":"Positions and relationships expressed in degrees (90°, 60°, 30°, 180°) and clock-hour divisions rather than adjacent/opposite/left-right language. Requires converting angular relationships to hour-position arithmetic (e.g. 90° = 3 hour-divisions apart), a distinct sub-skill from standard circular position lookup."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E2B_Q012","question_text":"Who is seated opposite Q?","options":{"A":"J","B":"S","C":"M","D":"U"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"S","solution_text":"S sits opposite Q.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q013","question_text":"If K is seated at 7th hour division then who is seated at the 9th hour division of the clock?","options":{"A":"O","B":"J","C":"M","D":"Q"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"O","solution_text":"If K is seated at 7th hour division of the clock then O is seated at 9th hour division of the clock.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q014","question_text":"What is the angle between L and M?","options":{"A":"60°","B":"90°","C":"30°","D":"180°"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"90°","solution_text":"The angle between L and M is 90°.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E2B_S15_18","exercise":"exercise_2b","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":true,"setup_text":"The following questions are based on the information given below: Eight persons G, H, I, J, K, L, M, and N are sitting around a square table. Some of them are facing the centre and others are facing away from the centre. (i) I is sitting at one of the corners and is facing away from the centre. (ii) Neither J nor M is a neighbour of I. (iii) The neighbours of I face the same direction as I. (iv) L and M face the same direction and sit opposite each other. (v) H is to the immediate right of M, and G is to the immediate left of I. (vi) J and K are neighbours of N, and face different directions. (ix) N sits opposite [G] and faces the centre. (x) M and K are facing different directions, and K is to the immediate right of N.","setup_solution":"From (i), I is sitting at one of the corners and facing away from the centre. From (iii), the neighbours of I face the same direction as I, hence they face away from the centre. From (ix), the one who sits opposite I faces a different direction from I, hence faces the centre. From (iv), L and M face the same direction and sit opposite each other. From (vii)/(v), G is to the immediate left of I. From (ii), neither J nor M is a neighbour of I. Assume L and M face away from the centre and L is to the immediate left of G; H is to the immediate right of M — but placing H there blocks placing J and K as neighbours of N. Hence L and M must be facing the centre. From (x), K must be facing away from the centre. From (vi)/(viii), J must be facing the centre. Final arrangement (square table, 8 positions — 4 corners + 4 sides): G, I, L, M, J, N, K, H going around, with I/G/L neighbours facing away from centre and the N-opposite-G group facing centre as derived.","entity_count":8,"variables":2,"constraints":7,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","table_geometry":{"shape":"square","seats":8},"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["multi_constraint_balancing","trial_and_error_verification"],"type_specific":{"note":"Square table geometry with corner vs side seat distinction (relevant to which seats count as 'neighbours'). Facing direction tracked as second variable, with a derived rule (neighbours of I face same direction as I; person opposite I faces differently) that must be inferred rather than being a directly stated clue."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E2B_Q015","question_text":"How many persons are facing away from the centre?","options":{"A":"Five","B":"Four","C":"Two","D":"Three"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Four","solution_text":"Four persons are facing away from the center.","question_operation":"count_arrangements","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q016","question_text":"Three of the four are alike in a certain way and so form a group. Which is the one that does not belong to that group?","options":{"A":"JH","B":"NI","C":"MK","D":"MN"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"MN","solution_text":"Except MN, in all other pairs JH, NI and MK, both are facing different directions.","question_operation":"odd_one_out","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q017","question_text":"Who is sitting to the immediate right of H?","options":{"A":"M","B":"G","C":"I","D":"K"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"M","solution_text":"M is sitting to the immediate right of H.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q018","question_text":"Who is sitting in the opposite position of G?","options":{"A":"H","B":"M","C":"K","D":"J"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"K","solution_text":"K is sitting opposite G.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75}]},{"set_id":"LR_E2B_S19_21","exercise":"exercise_2b","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. There are two circular tables in a room. Six Russians – A, B, C, D, E and F – are sitting at one table and six Frenchmen – M, N, O, P, Q and R – are sitting at the other table. A and D are sitting opposite to each other. B and E are sitting opposite each other. C sits to the right of D. B is the only person who can translate Russian to French; C is the only person who can translate French to Russian, and none of them does the vice-versa, unless so stated. E and F sit adjacent to each other. Also, M sits opposite P; Q sits to the right of R and R sits opposite O. N and P sit adjacent to O. On the table, any person can talk to another person, only as stated below. The only conversations that took place are as given below: A spoke to B; B to R; R to C; R to Q; Q to P; P to O; O to N; N to M; C to D; D to E; F to A; M to R; and E to F. The conversations are one-sided i.e., A spoke to B implies that A is the speaker and B is the listener and not vice-versa.","setup_solution":"Russian Table (circular, 6 seats): A opposite D, B opposite E, C to the right of D, E adjacent to F — final order clockwise: A, B, C, D, E, F. French Table (circular, 6 seats): M opposite P, Q to the right of R, R opposite O, N and P adjacent to O — final order clockwise: M, N, O, P, Q, R. The directed conversation chain given is: A→B, B→R, R→C, R→Q, Q→P, P→O, O→N, N→M, C→D, D→E, F→A, M→R, E→F. This forms a directed graph spanning both tables (B and C act as the only bridge/translator nodes connecting the two tables).","entity_count":12,"variables":1,"constraints":13,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","table_geometry":{"shape":"circular_dual_table","seats":"6+6"},"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["network_traversal","linear_sequencing"],"type_specific":{"two_table_problem":true,"translator_bridge_nodes":["B","C"],"note":"Two independent circular seating arrangements (one per table) combined with a directed conversation graph linking them through two bridge nodes (B translates Russian-to-French, C translates French-to-Russian). The core difficulty driver is tracing message paths through the directed graph, not the seating arrangements themselves, which are each independently simple once resolved."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E2B_Q019","question_text":"If C wants to send a message to M, then how many persons must the message pass through? (excluding the first and the last)","options":{"A":"1","B":"6","C":"10","D":"None of these"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"10","solution_text":"C to M is: C → D → E → F → A → B → R → Q → P → O → N → (M) = Total of 10 persons.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":180},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q020","question_text":"If the order of conveying messages is reversed at both the tables (i.e., \\"A speaks to B\\" now becomes \\"B speaks to A\\", and so on) and also B and C exchange their interpretory skills, then which of the following must be true?","options":{"A":"A can send a message to Q involving only 2 persons.","B":"The person sitting to the right of F can send a message to the person sitting to the left of N, by involving only five persons.","C":"The person sitting to the right of C, on the same table, can translate Russian into French.","D":"The maximum number of persons involved in the longest message in this new arrangement is more than that in the previous arrangement."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"The person sitting to the right of F can send a message to the person sitting to the left of N, by involving only five persons.","solution_text":"(A) The order of message sending will be as shown below: A → F → E → D → C → R → M → N → O → P → Q. As the number of persons involved is more than 2, hence this statement is false. (B) To the right of F is E, and to the left of N is O. The route will be as shown: (E) → D → C → R → M → N → (O). As there are 5 persons between E & O involved in passing the message hence this statement is true. (C) B sits to the right of C, who now translates French to Russian. Hence, this statement is false. (D) The maximum number of persons involved in message sending will remain the same in the new as well as the old arrangement hence (D) is false.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":240},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q021","question_text":"If the person sitting to the right of B wants to send a message to the person sitting two places to the left of D, then what is the maximum number of people involved between them? (excluding the two people)","options":{"A":"11","B":"10","C":"4","D":"None of these"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"10","solution_text":"A is sitting to the right of B and F is sitting two places to the left of D. The message from A to F can be sent in the following way: [A], B, R, Q, P, O, N, M, R, C, D, E, [F]. So, all the 12 persons are covered, subtract 2 from 12 (for A & F) and we get 10 persons involved in between A & F. Also in the route shown above, R is repeated twice, still the count is 10.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":180}]},{"set_id":"LR_E2B_S22_24","exercise":"exercise_2b","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Eight seats are arranged around a circular table and are numbered 1 through 8 in clock-wise direction. Eight persons – Parul, Madhu, Saugat, Krish, Lakshmi, Durga, Trishna and Nishi, are sitting in those seats not necessarily in that order. Whether or not Krish and Durga exchange their positions, neither of them is adjacent to either Parul or Lakshmi. If the person sitting in seat number 1 and Saugat exchange their positions, then Saugat will be adjacent to Madhu. Even if Nishi and Trishna exchange their positions, Durga will be adjacent to Trishna. No person is adjacent to both Parul and Lakshmi. No person is adjacent to both Durga and Krish.","setup_solution":"From the statement that \\"Even if Nishi and Trishna exchange their positions, Durga will be adjacent to Trishna\\", we get that Durga is sitting adjacent to both Nishi and Trishna. As no person is adjacent to both Krish and Durga, neither Nishi nor Trishna is adjacent to Krish. The possible cases are case (a), case (b), case (c) for the Durga-Nishi-Trishna block placement. As \\"if Krish and Durga exchange their positions, neither of them is adjacent to either Parul or Lakshmi,\\" Krish is adjacent to neither Parul nor Lakshmi. The possible positions for Parul and Lakshmi are derived per case. The remaining positions are for Madhu and Saugat. Since \\"if the person sitting in seat number 1 and Saugat exchange their positions, Saugat will be adjacent to Madhu,\\" Madhu is sitting either in seat number 2 or in seat number 8.","entity_count":8,"variables":1,"constraints":5,"case_splits":3,"resolution_type":"multiple","table_geometry":{"shape":"circular_numbered","seats":8},"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["case_elimination","trial_and_error_verification"],"type_specific":{"note":"Seats are numbered (not just relatively positioned), and several clues are phrased as counterfactual exchanges (\\"if X and Y exchange positions, then...\\") rather than direct statements, requiring the solver to reason about what an exchange would imply for current placement — an indirect inference layer distinct from direct adjacency/opposite clues."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E2B_Q022","question_text":"What is the maximum seat number in which Krish can sit?","options":{"A":"8","B":"7","C":"6","D":"4"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"7","solution_text":"As the numbering is done in clock-wise direction, in any of the above cases Krish cannot be on seat number 8. But if Madhu sits to the left of Krish, then Krish can be in seat number 7.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q023","question_text":"If Nishi is sitting in seat number 1, and Trishna is sitting to the immediate left of Parul, what is the seat number in which Saugat is sitting?","options":{"A":"2","B":"4","C":"3","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"4","solution_text":"As Madhu can only sit on either seat number 2 or 8, the only possible case is case (c). Saugat is sitting in seat number 4.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q024","question_text":"If Durga is sitting in seat number 2, then who is sitting to the right of Madhu?","options":{"A":"Krish","B":"Saugat","C":"Parul","D":"Lakshmi"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Krish","solution_text":"The condition is possible only in case (a) where the arrangement will be as follows. Krish is sitting to the right of Madhu.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150}]},{"set_id":"LR_E2B_S25_27","exercise":"exercise_2b","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. A family of eight persons – A, B, C, D, E, F, G and H –are sitting around a circular table, facing towards the centre, but not necessarily in the same order. The following information is known about their seating arrangement. F's son E, sits second to the left of B's son G. A's only daughter is three places away from E. Two persons are sitting between D's daughter and A's daughter. D's only daughter is not adjacent to any female and sits adjacent to A's son. D's father sits opposite A's son. C's only grand daughter is H who is the cousin of E. D's mother is not opposite any female, but sits adjacent to her son. F's mother - in - law is C whose only son is D. G's father F sits third to the right of D's mother and he is sitting opposite his wife.","setup_solution":"Given F's son E sits second to the left of B's son G. A's only daughter is three places away from E, placeable either third to the left or third to the right of F's son. Two persons sit between D's daughter and A's daughter, so A's daughter sits third to the right of F's son. D's daughter is not adjacent to any female and sits adjacent to A's son, so A's son sits to the immediate left of D's daughter (since D's father sits opposite A's son). D's mother is not opposite any female, so she sits opposite F's son's position area; G's father (F) sits third to the right of D's mother, confirming A's daughter is not D's mother, so D's mother sits opposite F's son. Final seating arrangement (clockwise): D(A's son), A's daughter, C(D's mother), B(A's daughter's position area), G(B's son), F(G's father), E(F's son), H(D's daughter) — derived per the family tree: C and A are a couple; D (son of C/A) and B are siblings with F as B's husband; H is D's daughter, E is F/B's son and cousin of H, G is F/B's son as well (sibling of E).","entity_count":8,"variables":2,"constraints":8,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","table_geometry":{"shape":"circular","seats":8},"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["relational_decoding","multi_constraint_balancing"],"type_specific":{"requires_relationship_decoding":true,"note":"Every clue is phrased through family relationships (son, daughter, father, mother, mother-in-law, cousin, granddaughter) rather than direct names, requiring full family-tree reconstruction before any seating position can be fixed. This is a deeper relational layer than the linear-arrangement family set, since here almost no clue gives a position without first resolving who someone is."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E2B_Q025","question_text":"Who sits second to the right of B's husband?","options":{"A":"A's son","B":"B's father","C":"G","D":"C"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"B's father","solution_text":"B's father sits second to the right of B's husband.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q026","question_text":"How many males are there in the family?","options":{"A":"Three","B":"Five","C":"Two","D":"Four"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Five","solution_text":"There are five males in the family.","question_operation":"count_arrangements","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q027","question_text":"Who is B's niece?","options":{"A":"E","B":"H","C":"B's daughter","D":"A's daughter"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"H","solution_text":"H is the niece of B.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E2B_S28_30","exercise":"exercise_2b","type":"circular_arrangement","is_set":true,"setup_text":"These questions are based on the following information. Eight persons P, Q, R, S, T, U, V and W sit around a circular table facing the center. P and Q are actors; R, S and T are directors; U and V are producers; W is a cinematographer. Also, there is an equal distribution of males and females among actors and producers. Further, the following information is known: 1. The two producers are neighbours of P. 2. T, the only male director, sits opposite P, who is female. 3. W sits between two directors. 4. There is a male sitting to the immediate right of T. 5. Q is neighbour of both the persons, a female producer and S.","setup_solution":"The given information tabulated: P-Female-Actor, Q-Male-Actor, R-Female-Director, S-Female-Director, T-Male-Director, U-Male/Female-Producer, V-Female/Male-Producer, W-Male-Cinematographer. Since a male sits to the immediate right of T, Q cannot sit to the right of T (as that would need checking), hence Q sits to the left of T. Final arrangement (clockwise): T(Director,M), P(Actor,F), Producer, Producer, [continuing] with W between two directors, and S adjacent to Q. Derived final order: T, P, V/U(producer), U/V(producer), W, S, R, Q — with W sandwiched between R and S (both directors), satisfying clue 3.","entity_count":8,"variables":3,"constraints":5,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","table_geometry":{"shape":"circular","seats":8},"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["multi_constraint_balancing","linear_sequencing"],"type_specific":{"attribute_layers":2,"note":"Three attributes tracked per person: position, profession (actor/director/producer/cinematographer), and gender. Profession groups are uneven in size (2,3,2,1), and gender distribution is only partially constrained (equal split stated only for actors and producers, not directors), adding asymmetry not seen in earlier profession-tracking sets."},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E2B_Q028","question_text":"Which of the following statements is true?","options":{"A":"Two directors of the same gender sit together.","B":"The number of males in each of the professions mentioned is more than the number of females.","C":"U, the producer, is neighbor of both P and Q.","D":"The persons sitting opposite each other are not of the same gender."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"The persons sitting opposite each other are not of the same gender.","solution_text":"Statement (A) is false. Statement (B) is false. There is only one male director. Statement (C) may be false as U may also be neighbour of P and R. Statement (D) is true. W – V/U, T – P and S – U/V.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q029","question_text":"S sits opposite ________","options":{"A":"U","B":"V","C":"R","D":"Cannot be uniquely determined"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Cannot be uniquely determined","solution_text":"S sits opposite U or V.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E2B_Q030","question_text":"Three of the following four are alike in a certain way and hence form a group. Find the one that does not belong to the group.","options":{"A":"VU","B":"TS","C":"WQ","D":"QP"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"WQ","solution_text":"Except option (C), the rest are male – female pairs belonging to the same profession.","question_operation":"odd_one_out","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]}]`);export{e as default};
